  "sections": [
    {
      "heading": "How each race names itself",
      "content": "<ul><li><strong>Elves</strong> use long, flowing names and often take an adult name when they come of age.</li><li><strong>Dwarves</strong> have a short personal name and a clan name, such as Stoneaxe or Goldvein.</li><li><strong>Halflings</strong> have short, friendly first names and homely family names drawn from the countryside.</li><li><strong>Tieflings</strong> carry either an infernal name or a \"virtue\" name for an idea they want to live up to, like Reverie or Verity.</li><li><strong>Dragonborn</strong> put the clan name first, then the personal name, so in Bhorrandesk Rhazkar the clan is Bhorrandesk.</li><li><strong>Orcs</strong> use short, hard names; <strong>gnomes</strong> collect several names and nicknames over a long life.</li><li><strong>Humans</strong> vary by region, which is why this list mixes Sword Coast names like Perrin Westwater with northern ones like Kjeld Greysail.</li></ul>"
    },
    {
      "heading": "Picking a name at the table",
      "content": "<p>Choose a name the other players can say. If the table keeps stumbling over Malthrenkazhar, they will call your dragonborn \"Mal\" by the second session, so you might as well plan a short form. A name that hints at the character's past (a halfling called Quickwater who can't swim) gives you something to role-play.</p><p>For a whole party, vary the first letters and lengths so names don't blur together in play: Vymira, Dagith and Ozzie are easier to keep apart than Vymira, Vireth and Velryna.</p>"
    }
  ],
  "faqs": [
    {
      "question": "What are good D&D names?",
      "answer": "Good D&D names fit the character's race and are easy to say at the table. Long flowing names suit elves (Selavyre, Thalvaris), sturdy names with a clan suit dwarves (Rurmund Stoneaxe), and homely ones suit halflings (Millie Quickwater)."
    },
    {
      "question": "What are good tiefling names?",
      "answer": "Tieflings use either an infernal-sounding name, such as Morthazel, Vexathos or Ulzira, or a virtue name that describes an idea, such as Reverie, Verity or Threnody."
    },
    {
      "question": "How do dragonborn names work?",
      "answer": "Dragonborn give their clan name first and their personal name second, because the clan matters more to them than the individual. Childhood names are often used among clutchmates as well."
    }
  ],
